(d) Provide health education on hygiene, nutrition, and disease prevention to at least 100,000 individuals; and

(e) Strengthen community-based referral pathways to secondary health facilities.

2.5 The Contractor has demonstrated capacity and experience in delivering community-based health outreach services in Cross River State and has been selected to support the Ministry in achieving the Program objectives.

2.6 The Parties have agreed to enter into this Contract for the provision of the Services, on the terms and conditions set out below.

---

3. GENERAL OBLIGATIONS OF THE PARTIES

3.1 Obligations of the Contractor

The Contractor shall:

(a) perform the Services with due diligence, skill, and care, in accordance with the highest professional standards and best practices in community health outreach;

(b) comply with all applicable laws, regulations, and guidelines of the Federal Republic of Nigeria and Cross River State, including the National Primary Health Care Development Agency (NPHCDA) protocols;

(c) ensure that all personnel engaged in the delivery of the Services are appropriately qualified, trained, and vetted;

(d) maintain a register of all personnel deployed under this Contract, including their qualifications and roles;

(e) cooperate fully with the Ministry, its Authorised Officer, and any designated monitors or auditors;

(f) provide the Ministry with all information and documentation reasonably required to verify performance of the Services;

(g) take all reasonable steps to ensure the safety and well-being of community members during outreach sessions;

(h) handle all health commodities and equipment with care, and in accordance with manufacturer instructions and Ministry guidelines;

(i) report immediately any serious incident, adverse event, or safety concern arising during the delivery of the Services;

"Services" means the community health outreach activities, including the delivery of primary healthcare services, health promotion, and referral pathways as described in Schedule 1.

1.2 Words importing the singular shall include the plural and vice versa. Headings are for convenience only and shall not affect the interpretation of this Contract.

1.3 References to Clauses and Schedules are references to clauses of and schedules to this Contract.

---

**2. SCOPE OF SERVICES**

2.1 The Contractor shall provide comprehensive community health outreach services within the Service Areas, in accordance with the detailed description set out in Schedule 1.

2.2 The Services shall include, but not be limited to:

(a) Provision of primary healthcare consultations and treatment for common ailments;
(b) Immunization services for children and pregnant women;
(c) Antenatal and postnatal care;
(d) Family planning services and counselling;
(e) Health education and sensitization on disease prevention, hygiene, and nutrition;
(f) Screening for hypertension, diabetes, and other non-communicable diseases;
(g) Referral of complex cases to secondary or tertiary health facilities;
(h) Community-based data collection and reporting.

2.3 The Contractor shall perform the Services with all due diligence, efficiency, and in accordance with the standards of the Nigerian healthcare profession and the policies of the Federal Ministry of Health.

---

**3. TARGET BENEFICIARIES AND SERVICE COVERAGE**

3.1 The Program is designed to reach a defined population across the Service Areas.

3.2 The contract states that Rural Outreach Program 2024 target_beneficiaries is 50000 people. Record status: current. This figure represents the aggregate number of unique individuals to be reached with at least one service intervention over the full duration of the Program, as measured against the performance indicators in Schedule 4. This target is the current and operative target for the Program and shall not be reduced without a formal amendment to this Contract.

3.3 The target beneficiary population is distributed across the Service Areas as indicated in Schedule 1. The Contractor shall use reasonable efforts to ensure equitable access to services across all communities within the Service Areas.

3.4 For the avoidance of doubt, a "unique individual" is a person who receives a service and is recorded with a unique identifier in the Program's data management system. Repeat visits by the same individual shall not be counted as new beneficiaries for the purpose of the target in Clause 3.2.

3.5 The Contractor shall maintain a beneficiary register, in both paper and electronic format, which shall be available for inspection by the Ministry or its designated monitors at all reasonable times.

---

**4. PERFORMANCE INDICATORS AND REPORTING**

4.1 The Contractor's performance shall be measured against the indicators set out in Schedule 4. The primary indicators include:

(a) Number of unique beneficiaries reached (target as per Clause 3.2);
(b) Number of immunization sessions conducted;
(c) Number of antenatal visits attended;
(d) Number of health education sessions conducted;
(e) Percentage of referred cases that are successfully followed up;
(f) Data completeness and timeliness of reporting.

4.2 The Contractor shall submit monthly progress reports to the Ministry’s Monitoring and Evaluation Unit, using the templates provided in Schedule 5. Reports shall be submitted no later than the 5th day of the following month.

4.3 Quarterly performance review meetings shall be held between the Ministry and the Contractor to assess progress against the indicators. The Contractor shall provide a presentation and supporting data for these meetings.

4.4 The Ministry reserves the right to conduct independent verification of the reported figures, including spot checks and community-level validation exercises.

4.5 The Contractor shall maintain a database of all services provided, which shall be updated continuously and backed up weekly. The data shall be disaggregated by age, sex, and community, as per the Ministry's data standards.
